UDP PING (-sn)
 Finally, we can use UDP to discover if the host is online. Unlike a TCP SYN ping, sending a UDP
packet to an open port is not expected to elicit a reply. However, if we send a UDP packet to a
closed UDP port, we expect to get an ICMP port-unreachable packet; this indicates that the
target system is up and available.
.
.
nmap -PU -sn 'machine ip' ->
The syntax to specify the ports is similar to that of TCP SYN ping and TCP ACK ping
.
