Reverse DNS (rDNS) (-R)
is the process of resolving an IP address to a hostname (the opposite of normal DNS). 
Instead of asking “What is the IP of example.com?”, you ask “What domain name is assigned to 10.200.6.15?”
.
nmap -R 'machine ip' -> 
In Nmap, using -R forces reverse lookups for all discovered hosts, while -n disables them. This helps identify system roles (e.g., mail.company.local, dc01.domain.com)
However, rDNS records aren’t always configured or accurate,so results may be missing or misleading.
By default, Nmap will look up online hosts; however, you can use the option -R to query the DNS server even for offline hosts. 
.

